              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "What do you notice?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There are no points of intersection because $\\Delta=-3<0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}-x+1&=\\frac{1}{x}\\\\-x^2+x&=1\\\\x^2-x+1&=0\\\\\\Delta&=(-1)^2-4(1)(1)\\\\&=-3<0\\end{align*}$$\nThere are no real solutions, so the graphs have no points of intersection.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Sketch the two graphs on the same axis to visualise the scenario.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The graphs of $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $y=-x+1$ do not intersect.",
          "worked": "Sketch $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ with branches in quadrants I and III and asymptotes $x=0$ and $y=0$. Draw $y=-x+1$ through $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$. The graphs do not intersect.",
